{
  "an10.55:0.1": "Numbered Discourses 10.55 ",
  "an10.55:0.2": "6. Your Own Mind ",
  "an10.55:0.3": "Decline ",
  "an10.55:1.1": "There Sāriputta addressed the mendicants: ",
  "an10.55:1.2": "“Reverends, mendicants!” ",
  "an10.55:1.3": "“Reverend,” they replied. ",
  "an10.55:1.4": "Sāriputta said this: ",
  "an10.55:2.1": "“Reverends, they speak of an individual liable to decline, ",
  "an10.55:2.2": "and one not liable to decline. ",
  "an10.55:2.3": "But how did the Buddha define an individual liable to decline, and one not liable to decline?” ",
  "an10.55:2.4": "“Reverend, we would travel a long way to learn the meaning of this statement in the presence of Venerable Sāriputta. ",
  "an10.55:2.5": "May Venerable Sāriputta himself please clarify the meaning of this. ",
  "an10.55:2.6": "The mendicants will listen and remember it.” ",
  "an10.55:3.1": "“Then listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” ",
  "an10.55:3.2": "“Yes, reverend,” they replied. ",
  "an10.55:3.3": "Sāriputta said this: ",
  "an10.55:4.1": "“How did the Buddha define an individual liable to decline? ",
  "an10.55:4.2": "It’s when a mendicant doesn’t get to hear a teaching they haven’t heard before. They forget those teachings they have heard. They don’t keep exercising the teachings with which they are already familiar. And they don’t come to understand what they haven’t understood before. ",
  "an10.55:4.3": "That’s how the Buddha defined an individual liable to decline. ",
  "an1